          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been tired and light-headed for months, my heart races on stairs, and my periods are heavy. Is that urgent, what could iron deficiency do to the blood, and which ICD-10 chapter covers anemia?",
          "answer": "Your symptoms are reported as routine by a risk assessment tool. However, if your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice (diagnostic).\n\nThe symptoms you describe could be consistent with several conditions. For example, hypothyroidism can cause fatigue, heavy periods, and a slowed heart rate, although you mentioned a racing heart. Generalized anxiety disorder can also cause fatigue, light-headedness, and a racing heart (diagnostic).\n\nIron deficiency can lead to anemia, meaning your blood may have fewer red blood cells, or these cells may be smaller and carry less oxygen than usual. This can result in symptoms such as fatigue, breathlessness, dizziness, and heart palpitations (racing heart) (condition-iron-deficiency-anemia).\n\nAnemia is covered in Chapter 3 of the ICD-10-CM, titled \"Diseases of the blood and blood-forming organs and certain disorders involving the immune mechanism\" (D50-D89). Various types of anemia are classified within this chapter (coding-icd10-chapter-03-blood).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
